until the bouncing muffin face is gone
from the studies it seems that the total

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:03.160
amount of light energy hitting your skin
is the most important thing to look at

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:08.920
this is called the dose or flux and it's
measured in joules per square centimeter

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:13.270
to calculate how long you need to be
exposed to a light source to get that

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:18.190
particular dose I'm using the intensity
of the light or the irradiance which is

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:23.739
measured in watts per square centimeter
one watt is one Joule per second from

00:02:23.739 --> 00:02:27.670
the studies on human volunteers so far
it's a little tricky because different

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:31.570
experiments use different wavelengths of
light but we can get a ballpark figure

00:02:31.570 --> 00:02:35.530
from these studies you need at least 40
joules per square centimeter of light

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:40.750
for both visible and just blue light
it's not really useful to look at in

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:45.220
vitro studies where the skin cells were
in petri dishes since in real life your

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:48.940
skin isn't flat like that and there's
layers of dead skin protecting the

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:59.110
living cells underneath so real skin
should be able to handle more light next

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:02.709
let's look at how long it takes to get
this 40 joules per square centimeter

00:03:02.709 --> 00:03:07.420
amount from the Sun the reason we want
